<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><section class="snap"><h2>Louisville at a glance</h2>
<dl class="snap-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Jefferson County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Louisville Metro</dd></div><div><dt>Housing mix</dt><dd>Victorian mansions and brick shotgun houses fill the older urban neighborhoods, while postwar ranches, split-levels and newer subdivisions spread toward the eastern and southern parts of the county.</dd></div></dl>
<h3>Local claim risks</h3><ul class="tags"><li>Windstorm and straight-line wind</li><li>Hail on older roofs</li><li>Flash flooding and sewer backup</li><li>Tornadoes</li><li>Ice and frozen pipes</li></ul>
<h3>Neighborhoods and nearby communities</h3><ul class="tags light"><li>Highlands</li><li>Old Louisville</li><li>Crescent Hill</li><li>Germantown</li><li>St. Matthews</li><li>Clifton</li><li>Fern Creek</li><li>Okolona</li><li>Jeffersontown</li><li>Middletown</li></ul>
<h3>Weather history</h3><ol class="mini-tl"><li><b>1937</b><span>The Ohio River flood submerged much of the city and reshaped how Louisville built along the river.</span></li><li><b>1974</b><span>The April 3 Super Outbreak sent an F4 tornado across the city, damaging homes near Cherokee Park.</span></li><li><b>2008</b><span>Remnants of Hurricane Ike brought a September windstorm that knocked out power across the metro.</span></li><li><b>2009</b><span>An August flash flood dropped heavy rain in a short span, flooding basements and streets.</span></li></ol></section>
<h2>What Louisville Claims Usually Look Like</h2>
<p>Louisville sits in a spot where wind, hail and water all arrive with some regularity. Most disputes we see here are not about whether a loss is covered; they are about <strong>how much the repair really costs</strong>. A carrier’s desk estimate may price a patch where a full slope is needed, or skip the line items that a contractor working in a hundred-year-old house cannot avoid.</p>
<p>Wind and hail claims dominate after spring and summer storms. Water claims follow heavy rain, when combined sewers and older basement drains get overwhelmed. Those two patterns produce very different arguments, so we approach them differently.</p>
<h2>Older Housing, Harder Estimates</h2>
<p>Neighborhoods like Old Louisville, Germantown and the Highlands are full of plaster walls, tall ceilings, original trim and slate or tile roofs. Standard estimating software assumes modern drywall and asphalt shingles. When an adjuster prices a plaster ceiling as half-inch drywall, or ignores the labor of matching profiled millwork, the gap can be large.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Plaster versus drywall:</strong> replacing like kind and quality often means plaster or a veneer system, not a quick drywall hang.</li>
<li><strong>Matching:</strong> discontinued siding, brick and shingles raise the question of whether a whole elevation must be replaced to get a reasonable match.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades:</strong> rewiring, venting and ice-and-water requirements can apply once work is opened up, and many policies carry ordinance or law coverage worth checking.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Water Losses Near the River and in the Basement</h2>
<p>Louisville’s history with the Ohio River means many owners already know flood is excluded under a standard homeowners form. The more common fight is subtler: was it sewer backup, surface water, or a sudden pipe failure? Each is treated differently, and the answer drives whether appraisal can resolve the amount. Appraisal sets the value of a covered loss; it does not rewrite coverage. For a closer look, see our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/water-damage-appraisal/">water damage appraisal</a> page.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Before you invoke</b>Gather the carrier’s estimate, your contractor’s bid and photos taken before cleanup. A side-by-side comparison often shows the dispute is a handful of line items, not the whole claim.</div>
<h2>How We Work in Jefferson County</h2>
<p>We do not keep a storefront in Louisville; we come to the property. That means walking the roof when it is safe, measuring rooms, documenting materials and writing an estimate that a contractor could actually build from. If you are not sure whether appraisal makes sense yet, our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/second-look-estimate-review/">second-look estimate review</a> is a lower-cost way to find out where the real differences are before you send a demand letter.</p>
<p>Whatever the size of the loss, the goal is the same: an award that reflects what it actually costs to put a Louisville property back the way it was, priced at local labor and material rates rather than a regional average that never accounts for older construction, tight lots or limited access.</p>
<h2>Appraisal services available in Louisville</h2><p>Every Kentucky Insurance Appraiser service is available to owners in Louisville and across Jefferson County, including:</p><ul><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/party-appraiser-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/home-insurance-appraisal/">Homeowners Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/roof-hail-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/tornado-wind-appraisal/">Tornado &amp; Wind Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal/fire-smoke-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Louisville claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can appraisal help with a Louisville basement water claim?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Yes, if the water source is covered. Appraisal decides the amount of a covered loss. If the insurer says the water was flood or excluded sewer backup, that coverage question usually has to be resolved separately.</p></details><details><summary>Do you charge more for older homes in Old Louisville or the Highlands?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Fees depend on the size and complexity of the claim, not the neighborhood. Older homes often take longer to document because of plaster, trim and specialty roofing, and we explain that up front.</p></details><details><summary>How long does Louisville appraisal usually take?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><p>Many disputes resolve within a few weeks to a few months once both appraisers are named. Timing depends on how quickly the other side responds and whether an umpire is needed.</p></details></div>
